<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="trigonelline biosynthesis" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260304101735">
  <Description>&lt;b&gt;General Background&lt;/b&gt; |FRAME: METHYLNICOTINATE Trigonelline|, the &lt;i&gt;N&lt;/i&gt;-methyl conjugate of |FRAME: NIACINE|, was first isolated from seeds of fenugreek (|FRAME: TAX-78534|) |CITS: [JAHNS85]| and has been found since in many other plant species including pea, hemp, coffee, soybean and potatoes |CITS: [FODOR85]|. Trigonelline is often classified as a pyridine alkaloid |CITS: [DEWICK97]| |CITS: [SEIGLER98]|. As a direct derivative of nicotinic acid trigonelline belongs to the so-called &apos;pseudoalkaloids&apos; which may be the reason that other authors do not list this compound as an alkaloid but rank it more generic among nitrogen containing compounds |CITS: [HARBORNE93]|. Trigonelline is regarded as a physiological active compound in plants inducing leaf movements |CITS: [10777626]|, accumulating upon stress |CITS: [CHO99]| and acting as an osmoprotectant |CITS: [RAJASEKARAN01]|. Moreover, trigonelline has been found to function as a hormone that is involved in the control of the cell cycle in plants and animals. It was established that trigonelline was capable of promoting specific G2 cellular arrest |CITS: [TRAMONTANO82]|. The molecular reason for that seems to be a specific interference of trigonelline with the DNA replication process causing an elongated cell cycle and impair root elongation |CITS: [10923713]|. The G2 cellular arrest caused by trigonelline was found to depend on the age of the organism. Older seedlings of |FRAME:TAX-3888| produce an unusual substituted pyrrole that has been identified as an endogenous control factor that could override trigonelline induced cellular arrest |CITS: [LYNN87]|. &lt;b&gt;About This Pathway&lt;/b&gt; The biosynthesis of trigonelline has been investigated in connection with nicotinic acid (niacin) biosynthesis and NAD recycling in plants (similar to |FRAME:PYRIDNUCSAL-PWY| and |FRAME:PWY-5381|) |CITS: [ARDITTI79][ZHENG04]|. The N-methylation of nicotinic acid forming trigonelline is catalyzed by the nicotinate N-methyltransferase (EC 2.1.1.7) which has been first investigated in seeds of |FRAME:TAX-3888| |CITS: [13790768]|. This enzyme was later purified and characterized demonstrating a high preference for nicotinic acid as substrate but not for nicotinamide which has been often observed with partially purified enzymes |CITS: [3364975]|. Trigonelline has long been considered as a metabolic dead-end product but continuous research revealed that this nicotinic acid conjugate rather constitutes a kind of a storage form of niacin which can be re-converted to nicotinic acid, hence feeding the pyridine nucleotide cycle |CITS: [14029718]| |CITS: [6347195]|. The conversion back to nicotinic acid is due to a demethylating activity which is predominantly non-oxidative |CITS: [185134]| but the responsible enzyme and the methyl group acceptor remains to be characterized. However, the relatively slow demethylation of trigonelline to nicotinate has caused doubt on the notion of trigonelline being a true storage compound |CITS: [Upmeier88a]|. Instead, it was proposed that the formation of trigonelline may rather represent the detoxification of excess nicotinate and nicotinamide released from the pyridine nucleotide cycle |CITS: [15837705]|.</Description>
